      "text": "To solve this problem, we can use the fundamental principle of counting. This principle states that if there are \\(m\\) ways to do one thing, and \\(n\\) ways to do another, then there are \\(m \\times n\\) ways to do both.\n\nIn this case, Crystal has 4 entrees, 2 drinks, and 2 desserts. To find the total number of distinct possible meals, we multiply the number of choices for each category:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total meals} = (\\text{Number of entrees}) \\times (\\text{Number of drinks}) \\times (\\text{Number of desserts})\n\\]\n\nPlugging in the numbers:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total meals} = 4 \\times 2 \\times 2\n\\]\n\nNow, let's calculate this product:\n\n\\[\n4 \\times 2 = 8\n\\]\n\nThen:\n\n\\[\n8 \\times 2 = 16\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Crystal can buy 16 distinct possible meals from the given options.",
